WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.460
Not every curl request is a harmless read.

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:13.880
A script may create tickets, trigger deployments, approve workflows, upload artifacts, modify settings, rotate configuration, or delete records through an API.

3
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.020
That makes HTTP method choice and payload design operationally important.

4
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:24.580
Scripts should distinguish read-only requests from state-changing requests.

5
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:29.800
A status check and a production deployment trigger should not have the same validation threshold.

6
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:37.900
High-impact actions should require stronger endpoint validation, clearer intent, and safer failure behavior.

7
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.420
Request bodies should be built safely.

8
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:47.880
Payloads should match the content type the API expects, and values inside the payload should be validated according to the workflow.

9
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:56.060
A malformed body can cause failure, but a subtly wrong body can cause a state change in the wrong environment or with the wrong parameters.

10
00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.180
Environment separation matters.

11
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.720
Development, staging, and production endpoints should be distinct and intentionally selected.

12
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:12.620
A script should not make it easy for a routine test run to send a state-changing request to production because of a loose environment variable or default.

13
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:18.660
Where an API and workflow support it, dry-run, preview, or approval steps can reduce risk.

14
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:28.040
The script should make intent clear before sending a request that changes server-side state, and it should log high-level outcomes without exposing sensitive payload details.
